<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nuruddin ar-Raniri</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1658">1658</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Nur al-Din al-Raniri</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nuruddin ar-Raniri was a Muslim scholar from Ranir in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> who worked at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a> from 1637 to 1644. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iskandar-thani">Iskandar Thani</a> he wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bustan-al-salatin">Bustan al-Salatin</a> and other Malay works, and he condemned the mystical teachings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamzah-fansuri">Hamzah Fansuri</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syamsuddin-of-pasai">Syamsuddin of Pasai</a> as heretical, having their books burned. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1644 and died there in 1658.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nuruddin-ar-raniri.jpg" alt="Nuruddin ar-Raniri" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author from book Hamzah Fansuri dan Nuruddin ar-Raniry by Dr. Edwar Djamaris and Drs. Saksono Prijanto · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nuruddin_ar-Raniry.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
